@startuml Topic7_Topologies
' ============================================================
' TOPIC 7: Topologies (the shape of the multi-agent system)
' Selection test: "Is there a boss?" / "Fixed sequence?" /
' "Independent equals?"
' ============================================================
title Topic 7 — Topologies\n(The SHAPE of a multi-agent system — pick by the shape of the problem)

skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 12

start

:Designing a multi-agent system —\nwhich topology?;

if (Is there a BOSS?\n(one agent directs the others)) then (YES)
  #LightGreen:**HUB-AND-SPOKE**;
  note right
    Central hub coordinates the spokes.
    + Centralized control
    + Clean AUDIT TRAIL
    - Single point of failure (the hub)
    - Bottleneck at the hub
    - Context BLOAT at the hub
    (This is the Orchestrator pattern, Topic 5.)
  end note
else (NO boss)
  if (FIXED SEQUENCE of steps?\n(order matters, each feeds the next)) then (YES)
    #LightBlue:**PIPELINE**;
    note right
      Agents in a line: output -> next input.
      + Modular, swappable stages
      + Easy to reason about
      - Latency = SUM of all stages
      - CASCADE failure (one break stops the line)
    end note
  else (NO fixed order — independent equals)
    #Orange:**PEER-TO-PEER (P2P)**;
    note right
      Agents talk directly, no coordinator.
      + Low latency
      + Resilient (no single point of failure)
      - Hard to AUDIT
      - Emergent / unpredictable behavior
    end note
  endif
endif

stop

floating note left
  == 3-QUESTION SELECTION TEST ==
  1. Is there a boss?         -> HUB-AND-SPOKE
  2. Fixed sequence of steps? -> PIPELINE
  3. Independent equals,
     no boss / no order?      -> PEER-TO-PEER
  ....
  == EVALUATOR-OPTIMIZER pattern ==
  Generator produces -> Evaluator critiques -> loop.
  Termination condition is NOT optional here
  (or it loops forever — see Topic 4).
  ----
  == ANTI-PATTERNS ==
  * Hub-and-spoke with no real hierarchy (needless hub)
  * P2P where an audit trail is REQUIRED
  Hybrids are valid IF each handoff is explicitly designed.
end note

@enduml
